This method processes the log entries from multiple compressed text files or zip archives and returns a summary report containing the frequency of unique log levels and the total number of log entries per minute. The method handles any exceptions gracefully, skips malformed or non-conforming entries, and ensures efficient memory usage.

To support real-time logging, you can use Java's built-in `ServerSocket` and `Socket` classes to establish a network socket for receiving new log entries. You can also integrate the logging system with an external analytics service using HTTP requests or WebSockets.

For detecting and handling log entry duplication, you can maintain a set of unique log entries and check each incoming entry against this set before processing it.

To optimize memory usage and minimize garbage collection overhead, consider using Java's `ByteBuffer` and `ByteArrayOutputStream` classes for handling large log entries. You can also implement your own caching mechanism to store frequently accessed data.

For scaling horizontally, you can use a load balancer like Apache httpd or HAProxy to distribute incoming log entries evenly across multiple instances of your logging system.']

